2. Avoid drinks that cause dehydration, such as: Alcohol or coffee, tea, caffeinated and sugary drinks.
3: Do not eat salty and sweet by reducing the intake of salt and sugar. Eat fruits that are rich in fiber and water, as well as natural fruits. Avoid foods high in protein, salt, sugar and fat.
4: Wear clothes that help protect against heat and prevent sunburn, such as wearing light-colored loose clothing, can use sunscreen to help and wear a hat with a wide brim or use an umbrella as a heat shield. Day and often change clothes if it is too wet.
5: Avoid dressing your baby or toddler with too thick clothing or wrapping them in a blanket to keep them warm.
6: Avoid direct sunlight for too long (seek shade or use sunscreen) and reduce the amount of sunlight entering the house for too long and reduce the heat entering the house.
8: Take cold showers or plain water often, but avoid causing extreme temperature changes, avoid taking cold showers immediately after returning from hot temperatures, which can cause hypothermia and Dangerous, especially to the elderly or children.
9: If you go outside, schedule outdoor activities for early morning if possible or in the evening when the temperature drops to cool, avoid activities at noon or when the sun is hot. Gradually adapt to hot weather conditions.
10: Know your limits and your own ability to work safely in the heat that can be tolerated, work slowly, do not rush to spend a lot of energy with a sudden and very hot.
11. Take time to relax and take shelter in a shady, cool place or air conditioner, or stay indoors or in a cool place as much as possible, unless you are sure your body can withstand the heat. Any level.
12: Rely only on the fan as your main cooling device During the heat, use other means to reduce the heat.
13. Limit the use of stoves and ovens - it will make you and your home warmer.
14: Family members should be diligent in monitoring and monitoring the vulnerable to hot weather, including the elderly, 65 years old, the sick, children under 5 years old, the chronically ill and the sick. Psychologically, because they may need help at least twice a day during hot weather, do not leave them unattended.
15. If you have symptoms such as dizziness, nausea or vomiting, headache, muscle cramps (crocodile cramps are the first sign of the effects of heat, move to a cool place, rest for 2-5 minutes and drink cold water slowly, do not continue activities because There may be a high risk of avoiding being surrounded by people with health problems who should seek immediate medical attention.
