China will make every effort to stabilize exports (video inside) (Beijing) - Chinese state media reported on Friday, quoting the Chinese premier that China must "try every means" to stabilize its exports. It goes to developed countries as a sign that the impact of a possible global economic downturn on the Chinese economy remains a concern.
Reuters reports that trade flows to and from the world's second-largest economy continued to decline as the Chinese government released the latest data in March on exports. Imports fell 6.8 percent and imports fell 10.2 percent for the January-February period from a year earlier.
At a cabinet meeting on Friday, Premier Li was quoted as saying that practical and effective measures would be put in place at the appropriate time to stabilize market expectations, as well as take measures to guide enterprises to strengthen exploration. Developing countries and regional markets to deepen.
Other Asian exporters, such as South Korea and Vietnam, also saw their exports fall in the first few months of 2023, according to Reuters. It is not the only country facing its export problems.
A 26.5 percent drop in China's semiconductor imports for January-February indicates a slump in electronics exports due to higher inflation in the United States and Europe. Interrupt demand from China.
China will release the next trade data on April 13.
