(Pursat): Thorn Khak is a young man living in a remote area in O'Som village, O'Som commune, Veal Veng district, Pursat province, who struggled until he finished high school (Bakdup) in his hometown.
Speaking to the media at the opening of the second phase of the Zero Trap Campaign in Pursat on April 7, 2023, Thorn Khak stated that when applying for a scholarship, the organization has no limit, but when Graduates must come back to help the community for 3 years, so we need to use the knowledge and skills learned to help develop the villages and communes according to the skills to change people not to enter the forest, hunting and logging.
"The Wildlife Alliance (WA) Scholarship is very good, it pays for schools, accommodation, water, and $ 150 a month to use both health insurance and health insurance," said Thorn Khak. Cutting three clothes a year, shoes, bags, school supplies.
With the scholarship from the Wildlife Alliance, Thorn Khak claims that the scholarship recipients are warm and diligent in learning the skills they choose to contribute to their local development with the authorities and the government.
Thorn Khak was also very proud after receiving a scholarship from the Red Cardamom South Cardamom Reds project from Wildlife Alliance, while no one in his community has received this scholarship to have the opportunity to continue. Study in college.
"Without this scholarship, I would not be able to pursue a four-year bachelor's degree in Phnom Penh. I will do my best to learn skills to local development to explain to people not to go into the forest, logging and hunting. " This is the additional emphasis of the young Thorn Khak.
Thorn Khak thanked the Red Plus Southern Cardamom Mountains project, which is owned by the Ministry of Environment, which provides scholarships to poor students in protected areas of scarce natural resources who can pursue higher education in their dreams.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, said that the improvement of natural resource conservation has enabled Cambodia to sell carbon credits to foreign companies to fund the protection of forests and partly to develop the local economy.
In addition, Mr. Neth Pheaktra continued that through the Carbon Credit Project, scholarships have been provided to the children of the people in the natural resource protection areas to continue their higher education at various colleges in Phnom Penh.
REDD + is a national program to reduce greenhouse gas emissions from deforestation and forest degradation. REDD + aims to protect forests for the national interest and contribute to global climate change mitigation under the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change.
The REDD + Plus Cardamom Mountains project was organized by the Ministry of Environment and the Wildlife Alliance for three purposes:
* First: To help improve the livelihood of local people * Second: To protect the forest to survive because the forest is a storehouse of carbon for sale * Third: To bring revenue to the Royal Government Cambodia.
