In one year, more than 100 forest and wildlife crimes were cracked down in Pursat province
(Pursat): Mr. Kong Puthyra, Director of the Pursat Provincial Department of Environment, said that 108 cases of forest and wildlife crimes were cracked down in 2022, two of which were sent to court.
Pursat Province has 6 protected areas: South Cardamom Mountain National Park, Central Cardamom Mountain National Park, Phnom Samkos Wildlife Sanctuary, Phnom Oral Wildlife Sanctuary, Tonle Sap Lake Multi-use Area and Biodiversity Conservation Corridor. It has a total area of 689,297 hectares.
On the occasion of the opening of the second phase of the Zero Trap Campaign on April 7, 2023 in Pursat town, Mr. Kong Puthyra confirmed that the rangers had done their duty by patrolling and collecting traps and confiscating 106 hunting equipment. Case.
Mr. Kong Puthyra said that eating wild animals and using samples as furniture is still a habit of local people.
The Director of the Pursat Provincial Department of Environment welcomes and fully supports the Zero Trap Campaign now, as Pursat is a province in the northwest of Cambodia, which is geographically rich in natural resources and rich in biodiversity. Many species of wildlife, such as elephants, rhinos, bears, bears, deer, wild boar, etc., and the Tonle Sap Lake has many species of fish.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Chairman of the Zero Trap Campaign, stated that the second phase of the Zero Trap Campaign aims to strengthen the protection and conservation of natural resources, prevent illegal wildlife trade, strengthen law enforcement, strengthen the local economy. In order to reduce the number of traps in the natural resource protection area, and change the attitude of people to stop eating wild meat.
It should be noted that Pursat province has been jointly launched by the Ministry of Environment and civil society organizations to launch the second phase of the Zero Trap Campaign among the provinces bordering the Cardamom Mountains, including Kampong Speu, Koh Kong, Battambang, Pailin and Siem Reap to save wildlife. Facing extinction while trapping is still a hidden killer and has been a serious threat to wildlife survival.
