Cambodia's success in selling carbon credits has boosted Cambodia's national prestige on the international stage that not many countries in the world can and has tackled with Cambodia's defamation campaign. The largest forest in the world.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra made this remark when he chaired a press conference on the second phase of the Zero Trap Campaign in Pursat province on April 6, 2023.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that after Cambodia achieved full peace, it created opportunities and opportunities to protect and conserve natural resources, which changed the context of Cambodia from logging for money but turning to conserving timber. To earn that carbon credit and through tourism sealed territory.
The spokesman confirmed that until now, Cambodia has sold carbon credits to major international companies with environmental responsibility, and the proceeds from the sale of carbon credits have been used to Strengthen the protection and conservation of natural resources and use them for economic development for local communities.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Environment confirmed that the proceeds from the sale of carbon credits will be distributed to local communities around wildlife sanctuaries across the country to help protect and conserve national and wildlife resources, especially contributing to the economy. People to eliminate old occupations, enter the forest, cut down trees, hunt animals and so on.
"This is an important turning point. Cambodia, now under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, has received revenue from its conservation and is eliminating the idea that only by deforestation can the country earn income from Protect and conserve its forests under the slogan "Keep trees standing for the benefit of the economy and society." This is an additional statement of Mr. Net Pheaktra.
