The ceremony was attended by Mr. and Mrs. Vice Chairman of the Traing District Local Party Working Group, Chairman and Deputy Chairman of the 14 Commune Party Working Group, Traing District Party Committee, 14 Commune Party Committees and party groups at Traing District Party Office. National Council for Social Protection inspects and updates the number of households vulnerable to inflationary pressures to receive government subsidies
(Phnom Penh): The National Council for Social Protection, Ministry of Planning and local authorities have inspected vulnerable families who are eligible for cash aid programs from vulnerable families during inflationary pressures to ensure that those at risk are real. Be sure to get this subsidy, which will help maintain the balance of life of the people on the poverty line, not fall below the poverty line.
He said that between December 2022 and April, the National Council for Social Protection and local authorities have done more work to verify and check to avoid gaps in the provision of subsidies.
He said that after the update, it was found that 1.5 million people across the country will be ready to pay for cash from vulnerable cash programs during the second round of inflationary pressures. On April 10, the state will spend about $ 18 million.
Please be informed that the cash subsidy program for vulnerable families during inflationary pressures was announced by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Head of the Royal Government of Cambodia, in accordance with Message No. 33SL, dated December 3, 2022.
The Cash Support Program for Vulnerable Families during Inflation Pressure is supported three times, the first: on December 10, 2022, the second: on April 10, 2023 and The third time: on July 10, 202. Understand business: President of the Microfinance Association of Cambodia tells existing borrowers not to worry, microfinance institutions do not raise interest rates
(Phnom Penh): The Chairman of the Board of Directors of the Microfinance Association of Cambodia emphasized that for those who are borrowing money from all microfinance institutions, the members of the Association do not have to worry about those institutions raising interest rates, despite the interest rates in the international market. Is growing, which is affecting the profitability of microfinance institutions.
Mr. Sok Voeun, Chairman of the Board of the Microfinance Association of Cambodia, told Fresh News that all the MFIs that are members of the Association will not raise interest rates for existing customers or those who are borrowing so as not to They have an additional burden on existing loans at a time when customers are experiencing a recession.
However, for new clients, each MFI may be in talks to raise interest rates to current market conditions. But will not rise, which can increase between 0.5% to 2% per year, depending on the type of customer, or can keep interest rates the same.
He added that the fact that MFIs do not raise interest rates is to ensure that customers are stable and do not have to pay extra on the rates paid with MFIs.
According to Sok Voeun, although the economic situation has not improved this year, he is not worried about the increase in non-performing loans, because in the last one or six months, the repayment situation Borrowed items do not seem to change and there are no bad signs, with repayments being normal.
In the context of the people's hardships in the aftermath of the current Kovid-19 crisis and the current economic crisis, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, has called on banks and microfinance institutions not to confiscate assets and to seize loans. And there are no real possibilities. However, Samdech urges those who have money to pay back the bank and microfinance, not to be overwhelmed by this appeal.
The microfinance sector in Cambodia has been playing an important role in contributing to economic and social development, especially the reduction of poverty in rural areas.
In fact, as of the end of the fourth quarter of 2022, the total assets of microfinance institutions and rural credit institutions totaled 44.4 trillion riels (equivalent to 10.8 billion US dollars), of which total loans were 38.9 Trillion Riel (equivalent to US $ 9.5 billion) provides more than 2.1 million customer accounts.
Deposit-taking microfinance institutions received a total of 19.4 trillion riels (equivalent to 4.7 billion US dollars) from about 2.7 million depositors.
